## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` or timeout |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException handler)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we decide whether to continue via the loop condition
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 5 seconds for graceful shutdown
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)))
        {
            // Thread did not exit — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent to Abort() in modern .NET.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes

1. **`volatile bool _running` → `CancellationToken`** — token is thread-safe by design and can interrupt blocking calls.

2. **`Thread.Abort()` → `_cts.Cancel()`** — cooperative signal, not a forced injection.

3. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`** — the `when` guard ensures you only catch your own cancellation.

4. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — the loop condition (`!token.IsCancellationRequested`) already controls continuation.

5. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `_workerThread.Join(timeout)`** — actually waits for the thread instead of a blind sleep.

### Critical Pitfall: Blocking Calls

Your `GetNextItem()` "may block." If it blocks indefinitely (e.g., `queue.Take()`), cancellation will never fire. You **must** pass the token into blocking calls:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocks forever, relies on Abort to interrupt
var item = _queue.Take();

// After (.NET 8) — wakes up on cancellation
var item = _queue.Take(token); // BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken)
```

### Other Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Consequence |
|---------|-------------|
| Not passing token to I/O or Sleep calls | Thread hangs; `Stop()` times out |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Timer/callback memory leak |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` guard | Accidentally swallowing unrelated cancellations |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint — design accordingly |
| No fallback if thread won't exit | Unlike `Abort()`, there's no force-kill for managed threads. If code truly can't cooperate, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` |

### If You Can't Modify `GetNextItem()`

If the blocking call is in code you don't control:

```csharp
// Run in a separate Task, abandon it on timeout
var workTask = Task.Run(() => GetNextItem());
var completed = await workTask.WaitAsync(token); // .NET 6+ API
```

This "abandons" the blocked call (it continues on its thread) but lets your loop proceed. Use sparingly — abandoned threads leak resources.
